        <div id="battery-vs-gas" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Battery Generator vs Gas Generator
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gas and battery generators answer the same question in opposite ways. A gas generator has modest efficiency but almost unlimited energy, because you can keep pouring fuel in. A battery generator has excellent efficiency and very clean power, but a fixed energy reserve that you refill from the grid, a car or solar panels. Our full breakdown is in <a href="/portable-power-station-vs-generator/">portable power station vs generator</a>; here is what matters when you are shopping specifically to replace a gas unit.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Running Watts vs Runtime</h3>

          <p>
            Running watts look similar on paper and are not. A 3,000W gas inverter and a 2,400W battery unit both run a fridge, a microwave and lights. The difference is how long. A gas generator with a gallon in the tank keeps going for hours at that load; a 2kWh battery at 1,500W lasts a little over an hour. Watts tell you what you can run at once. Watt-hours tell you for how long, and that number is what most battery shoppers underweight.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Surge</h3>

          <p>
            Surge is the second gap. Gas generators routinely carry starting ratings 20-30% above running watts, and the spinning engine has mechanical inertia that helps motors start. Battery inverters handle surge electronically and the duration is short, often milliseconds to a couple of seconds. Most modern units start refrigerators fine, but a well pump or a big window AC is where small battery generators fail and small gas generators succeed.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Battery vs Gas at a Glance</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">0 ppm</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Carbon monoxide from a battery unit</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">~85%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Usable share of rated Wh after inverter losses</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$0.50+</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Rough fuel cost per kWh from a small gas generator</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">3,000+</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Rated LiFePO4 cycles on most picks</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cost per Kilowatt-Hour</h3>

          <p>
            Cost per kilowatt-hour favors the battery once you own it. A small gas generator at light load commonly turns a gallon of gas into roughly 4-6kWh of electricity, which at $3-4 per gallon is somewhere around $0.50-1.00 per kWh before oil and maintenance. Recharging a battery from the grid costs whatever your utility charges, usually under $0.25 per kWh, and solar is free after the panels. The catch is purchase price: per watt of output, a battery generator costs several times more than a gas one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Noise, Fumes and Upkeep</h3>

          <p>
            Noise, fumes and upkeep are where battery wins outright. A gas inverter is commonly rated in the 50-60 dB range at 23 feet and a conventional open-frame unit is louder; a battery unit is silent until its cooling fan spins up. It produces no carbon monoxide, which is why it is the only kind you can run indoors, as we explain in <a href="/are-portable-power-stations-safe-indoors/">are portable power stations safe indoors</a>. There is no oil, no carburetor varnish and no stale fuel. The only maintenance is topping it up every few months.
          </p>

