        <div id="surge-motors" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Surge Watts and Motor Loads
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Motors Are the Problem</h3>

          <p>
            Motors are where battery generator plans fall apart. A refrigerator compressor, a sump pump, a well pump, a window AC or a furnace blower draws two to five times its running watts for a fraction of a second when it starts. A gas engine rides through that with the inertia of its spinning flywheel. A battery inverter has to deliver it electronically, and if the inrush exceeds what the inverter can push, it shuts down with an overload error.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Typical Motor Loads</h3>

          <p>
            Modern refrigerators are rarely a problem. A typical full-size fridge runs at 100-200W and starts at 600-1,200W, well inside any 1,500W-plus unit here. A 1/2 horsepower sump pump usually runs around 800-1,000W and can start at 2,000W or more, which is fine on the 1,800W-class units in most cases and comfortable on the AC200L. A 1 horsepower 240V well pump is a different animal and needs a 240V unit with serious surge headroom; see <a href="/what-size-generator-runs-a-well-pump/">what size generator runs a well pump</a> for the numbers.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Modes That Are Not Real Surge</h3>

          <p>
            Be careful with modes that sound like surge but are not. BLUETTI Power Lifting, EcoFlow X-Boost and Anker SurgePad let resistive loads such as kettles, toasters and space heaters run above the rated continuous output by lowering voltage. That trick works for heating elements and fails for motors, which need full voltage to start. Plan motor loads against the inverter's true continuous and surge ratings, not against the boost figure on the box.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Rule</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Start the biggest motor first, then add everything else.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A battery inverter handles one large inrush far better than an inrush on top of an existing load. Turn off other big appliances, start the pump or AC, wait for it to settle, then bring the fridge and microwave back.</p>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Measuring What a Device Draws</h3>

          <p>
            If you are not sure what a device draws, a plug-in watt meter costs less than a tank of gas and tells you running watts directly. For starting watts, our <a href="/generator-wattage-chart/">generator wattage chart</a> lists typical figures. Inverter-drive refrigerators, window units and mini-splits soft-start and draw far less inrush, which is one more reason a newer appliance pairs better with a battery.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="recharging" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Recharging: The Part Gas Owners Forget
          </h2>

          <p>
            Refueling a gas generator takes a minute. Recharging a battery generator takes somewhere between under an hour and a full day, depending on the source. That gap is the single biggest change for anyone switching from gas, and it is why recharge specs belong next to capacity and output on your checklist.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Wall Charging</h3>

          <p>
            Wall charging is fast on modern units. The Anker C1000 refills in about 58 minutes, the Jackery Explorer 1000 v2 in about an hour with emergency charging enabled, and the DELTA Pro 3 accepts up to 3,600W from a 240V source. That speed matters during rolling outages, when an hour of grid power between cuts can bring a unit back to full. Our guide on <a href="/how-long-does-it-take-to-charge-a-portable-power-station/">how long it takes to charge a portable power station</a> has the full math.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Solar Charging</h3>

          <p>
            Solar is the only recharge source in a multi-day grid-down outage, and it is slower than people expect. A 400W panel commonly produces something like 1.2-1.8kWh on a clear day once you account for sun angle, heat and wiring losses, and much less under clouds. That is enough to refill a 1kWh unit, about half of a 2kWh unit and roughly a third of a 4kWh unit. If you want solar to keep a fridge running indefinitely, plan for at least 800W of panels and read <a href="/how-to-charge-a-portable-power-station-with-solar/">how to charge a portable power station with solar</a> before you buy.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Car Charging</h3>

          <p>
            Car charging through a 12V socket is rarely worth it beyond phones; at roughly 100-120W, it would take most of a day to refill a 1kWh unit. Some brands sell alternator chargers that are much faster for people who live out of a vehicle, but for home backup, the realistic recharge sources are the grid, solar and, in the hybrid setup below, a small gas generator.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="predator" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Predator and Harbor Freight Power Stations
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Harbor Freight Sells</h3>

          <p>
            Harbor Freight sells its own line of Predator power stations alongside its gas generators. The company announced 350W, 600W, 1,200W and 2,000W models, describes them as using lithium iron phosphate cells, and sells them only in its own stores and online. Harbor Freight's listing for the Predator 2000 rates it at 2,000 running watts and about 1,545Wh of capacity, with roughly 1.5 hours to recharge from AC.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How It Compares</h3>

          <p>
            On paper that is competitive with the 1kWh-to-2kWh units on this list, and Harbor Freight's coupons often make the price attractive. What it does not have yet is the long track record, app ecosystem, expansion batteries and solar accessories of the brands we picked. Harbor Freight has also recalled some power station products in the past over a shock hazard, so check the CPSC recall list for the exact item number before buying any model, from any store.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Our View</h3>

          <p>
            Our view: a Predator power station is a reasonable buy if you already shop at Harbor Freight, want something in hand today and the coupon price is well below the equivalent EcoFlow, Anker, Jackery or BLUETTI. If you expect to expand capacity later, run 240V loads or rely on a well-supported app, the established brands are the safer long-term choice.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Other Generator Brands</h3>

          <p>
            The same logic applies to other generator brands that have moved into batteries. Westinghouse sells an iGen line of portable power stations, including the 1,008Wh iGen1000s rated at 1,500W continuous, through big-box stores. We could not verify a current DuroMax battery power station, so we have left that brand out rather than guess.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="where-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Home Depot, Lowe's and Where to Buy
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Battery Units on Big-Box Shelves</h3>

          <p>
            If you search for an electric generator at Home Depot or browse Lowe's generators, you will find battery power stations shelved right next to gas units, often labeled as solar generators or battery generators. Both stores carry EcoFlow, Jackery and Anker in many locations, plus house-adjacent brands such as Westinghouse. The advantage of buying in person is obvious during storm season: you can walk out with one the day before a hurricane.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Selection Trade-Off</h3>

          <p>
            The disadvantage is selection. Big-box stores typically stock a handful of popular sizes and often not the 240V units, expansion batteries or solar panels you may want later. Prices are usually matched across retailers during major sales, but online stores, including the manufacturers' own sites and Amazon, tend to carry every configuration and bundle, and they are where the deepest discounts on previous-generation models like the DELTA 2 appear.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Three Checks Before Checkout</h3>

          <p>
            Wherever you buy, check three things before checkout. First, the battery chemistry: LiFePO4 for anything that will sit in storage. Second, the return window, because you should test a unit against your real appliances within the first few weeks. Third, warranty length and who honors it; most of our picks carry five-year warranties when bought from authorized sellers, but third-party marketplace listings may not qualify.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hybrid" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. The Hybrid Approach: Battery Plus a Small Gas Generator
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most practical outage setup for a lot of homes is not battery or gas but both. A 1-2kWh battery generator handles the overnight hours and short outages silently, indoors, with no fumes. A small 2,000-3,000W gas inverter generator sits outside and runs for an hour or two at a time to refill the battery and cover the heaviest loads.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">It Cuts Fuel Use</h3>

          <p>
            This setup cuts fuel use sharply. A gas generator is least efficient at light load, which is exactly what it sees running a fridge overnight. Running it at moderate load to charge a battery for an hour, then shutting it off, uses fuel more efficiently and means the engine is not droning outside a bedroom window at 2 a.m. A unit with fast AC charging, such as the Anker C1000 or the DELTA Pro 3, makes the hybrid approach work best.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">It Lowers the Cost of Long Outages</h3>

          <p>
            It also lowers the cost of covering a long outage. Instead of a 4kWh battery generator and a large solar array, you can pair a 2kWh unit with a modest inverter generator and a few gallons of stabilized fuel. For a dual-fuel option that can also burn propane, see our guide to the <a href="/best-dual-fuel-generator/">best dual fuel generator</a>.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Safety Rule Still Applies</h3>

          <p>
            The safety rule does not change: the gas generator stays outdoors, at least 20 feet from doors and windows, with a battery-powered CO alarm inside the house. The battery generator is the part that comes inside. Never charge it from a generator running in a garage, even with the door open.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How to Size a Battery Generator
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: List the Loads</h3>

          <p>
            Start with the loads, not the product. List what you need to run, its running watts, its starting watts and how many hours a day it actually draws power. A refrigerator might be rated at 150W but only run its compressor 40-50% of the time, so plan around its average, not its peak. A CPAP is 30-60W without a heated humidifier. A router and modem are 15-25W together. LED bulbs are 8-10W each.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Check Output, Surge and Capacity</h3>

          <p>
            Add the running watts of everything you might run at once and pick a unit whose continuous output beats that with 20-30% headroom. Then check the largest single motor start against the surge rating. Finally, multiply each load's average draw by hours of use, add them up and divide by 0.85 to get the rated watt-hours you need. That number is your capacity target for one day without recharging.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Worked Example</h3>

          <p>
            Worked example: a fridge averaging 120W for 24 hours is 2,880Wh, a router at 20W is 480Wh, a CPAP at 50W for 8 hours is 400Wh and lights at 40W for 5 hours are 200Wh. That totals 3,960Wh, or about 4,660Wh rated after losses. One day with no recharge needs the DELTA Pro 3 class or an expanded 2kWh unit. Drop the fridge and the same household gets through a night on the EB3A plus a small top-up. Our <a href="/what-size-portable-power-station-do-i-need/">what size portable power station do I need</a> guide walks through more examples.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Matching the Result to a Pick</h3>

          <p>
            If the answer is under 1kWh and 1,500W, any of the 1kWh picks will do and you should choose on weight and recharge speed. If it is 1-2kWh, the AC200L or an expanded DELTA 2 fits. Past 3kWh or with any 240V load, look at the F3800 Plus or DELTA Pro 3. For a wider range of sizes and brands, including camping units, see our main guide to the <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">best portable power station</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a battery powered generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is a portable power station marketed as a generator: a large lithium battery, an inverter that outputs household AC power, and charging inputs for wall, car and solar. Unlike a gas generator it makes no fumes or engine noise, so it can be used indoors, but it stores a fixed amount of energy rather than producing power from fuel.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a battery generator replace a gas generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For short outages and essentials, yes. A 1-2kWh unit replaces a small 2,000-3,000W inverter generator for an evening or a night. To replace a 5,000-7,000W gas portable for a full day you need a 4kWh, 240V unit. For week-long outages with heavy loads, gas or a hybrid setup still makes more sense.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long will a battery generator run a refrigerator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Divide usable capacity by the fridge's average draw. At about 85% usable, a 1,024Wh unit has roughly 870Wh, so a fridge averaging 120W runs about 7 hours. A 2,048Wh unit runs it about 14 hours, and a 3,840Wh unit about 27 hours. Older or larger fridges draw more and shorten those times.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are battery generators safe to use indoors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. They produce no carbon monoxide, so they can run in a living room or bedroom, which a gas generator never can. Keep vents clear, avoid charging in extreme heat, use LiFePO4 models where possible and follow the manufacturer's spacing guidance. A gas generator used to recharge one must still stay outdoors.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which is better, Predator or EcoFlow power stations?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Harbor Freight's Predator line is priced aggressively and uses LiFePO4 cells, with a 2,000W model rated around 1,545Wh. EcoFlow offers a longer track record, a polished app, expansion batteries, 240V models and broad solar accessories. If you want expansion or 240V later, EcoFlow is safer; for a one-time coupon deal, Predator can be reasonable.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Home Depot or Lowe's sell battery generators?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Both shelve battery power stations next to gas generators, typically from EcoFlow, Jackery, Anker and Westinghouse, though stock varies by store. Big-box stores are useful for buying the day before a storm, while online retailers and manufacturer sites usually carry more sizes, 240V models, expansion batteries and solar bundles.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does a battery powered generator cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Small 250-300Wh units run roughly a couple hundred dollars, 1kWh units commonly sell in the several-hundred range on sale, 2kWh units cost more, and 4kWh 240V units run into the low thousands before expansion batteries. Per watt, that is several times a gas generator, offset by no fuel, no oil and far lower running costs.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing watts and ignoring watt-hours</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 2,400W battery unit and a 3,000W gas inverter run the same appliances, but the battery may last one evening while the gas unit runs all night on a tank. Always check the Wh figure and do the runtime math before deciding a battery generator can replace your gas generator.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Counting on boost modes for motors</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Power Lifting, X-Boost and SurgePad modes stretch output for heaters and kettles by lowering voltage. They do nothing for a well pump, sump pump or window AC trying to start. Size motor loads against the inverter's true continuous and surge ratings, not the boost number printed on the box.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming solar will keep up in a storm</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 400W panel might make 1.2-1.8kWh on a clear day and far less under the cloud cover that usually follows a storm. Solar extends runtime but rarely matches a running fridge plus a CPAP. Plan on a second recharge source, such as grid windows or a small gas generator, for multi-day outages.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a 120V unit for a 240V load</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most 240V well pumps, some sump systems and dryer-circuit transfer switches need split-phase power. No 120V battery generator can run them, no matter how large the battery. If any essential load is 240V, only the F3800 Plus or DELTA Pro 3 class units on this list will work.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing it empty for months</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A battery generator left at zero for a season can drop into a protective shutdown or lose capacity. Store it at roughly 50-80%, top it up every three to six months and run a short test with your real appliances before storm season, the same way you would exercise a gas generator.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

