        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For most households replacing a gas generator for short outages, the BLUETTI AC200L is the pick: 2,400W runs the essentials, 2kWh gets through a night and expansion batteries make it multi-day later. If you need 240V or a full day of runtime, step up to the Anker SOLIX F3800 Plus or EcoFlow DELTA Pro 3. If you only need a CPAP, lights and Wi-Fi, the EB3A does it for a fraction of the price.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Be honest about the limits. A battery generator is a tank of energy, not a fuel line, and the arithmetic of watt-hours decides how long it lasts. Size it to your real loads, plan a recharge source and keep a small gas inverter in the shed if your outages run for days. Done right, you get quiet, fume-free power for the outages you actually have.
